M. Kokaly (ranger5@u.washington.edu) Said something like:

: I guess I still can't understand how someone can fill up a 4 GB hard drive
: with essential information unless they are saving many many image files.
: Why not buy a Jaz drive or Syjet or even a Ls120 or Zip drive for data
: that you don't need that often?

Hmmm...  Maybe a single user, not really doing to much wouldn't.  I
collect up to around 10 or so data files in a day that are about 100K
each, just raw data.  Then to process it, and graph it, and eventually
take some out to put in slides for a presentation, just that alone starts
to add up.

Add in reports, and some cool applications (try to see just how much
Netscape 4.03 shipping Professional Edition for LINUX 2.0, and Star Office
take up, for just 2 commonly used applications!).  

Then throw Apache in there, and some web pages for a low (very low) volume
web server.  Then stuff like GIMP to do presentation graphics.  And, oh,
did I mention I am not the ONLY user of this box?  What happens when there
are over 3 or 4 accounts on it?  :)

LINUX is an awsome OS, and if I was only doing word processing and web
browsing, 1G would be plenty.  But, heck, I probably wouldn't even have to
use LINUX, that might as well be done in WIN95.  Oh, and by the way,
remember at all what the topic of the thread was?  Uh, I am guessing that
if someone is considering a Alpha or a Sparc, well, they might have more
than a few users, and they might want to run some big applications....

It's easy to use up all that room, without even trying.  You know, I still
go threw once a week and delete junk, just as a matter of housekeeping.
But drives still fill up.

Rob



