Marcus Maskos wrote:

> Hi !
>
> I am planning to 'replace' my good old 486/33 by a faster machine and
> so I took a look at systems based on 1 or 2 PPro(s)/200 with 256kB cache
> (with 512k the 'priceup' seems to be in no relation to the speedup :-).
> But my wish is to change from Intel to Alpha as it was a must
> to change from Microsoft to linux. Now I want to know if changing
> the hardware at this time is as easy and wise as changing the OS!

As you know the CPU performance is not the only parameter to take in
account. Which hardware to buy depends a lot on the kind of work you
normally do. If you will run heavy simulations or calculations an Alpha is
an excellent choice today. If you are a developer and spend most of the time
editing-compiling-debugging then a single Pentium with enough memory and a
SCSI harddisk will do it pretty well. If you want to use the machine as a
server then is better to look first at the kind of network you will install
and then find the hardware that better suites your needs.

In other words, there is not universal cure in the performance seek.

--
Carlos Vidal
carlos@tarkus.se






