In article <343129B3.1CED@ioc.ee>, Marko Vendelin <markov@ioc.ee> writes:
|> Hi,
|> 
|> I am installing Alpha PC164 with "generic" ne2000 compatible
|> network card. Unfortunately, the system (Linux)
|> detects it occasionaly: once everything works, the next time
|> it doesn't. IRQs are not conflicting.
|> 
|> My card is PCI NE2000. Does anybody fixed this problem?

The NE2000-driver works for my PCI-NE2000-clone (with the RTL8029 chip), but only
after a long debugging session. Some problems are x86-relicts (IO-ports declared
as short, works for ISA, but seldom for PCI...). The other point is a very
mysterious effect in the interrupt handler, if the interrupts are reenabled or
cleared. These effects always lead to a crash. The only way to get it to work is
not to enable interrupts and wait with the rti until the PCI-card has cleared its 
interrupts.
You can have my patches for 8390.c, maybe that works for you.

