On Tue, 30 Sep 1997, Marko Vendelin wrote:
> I am installing Alpha PC164 with "generic" ne2000 compatible
> network card. Unfortunately, the system (Linux)
> detects it occasionaly: once everything works, the next time
> it doesn't. IRQs are not conflicting.
> 
> My card is PCI NE2000. Does anybody fixed this problem?

I have lots of experience with NE2000's on PC's so everything I say may be
completely irrevelant to Alpha's, but several things come to mind: 

1.) if it's PCI, my understanding (quite possibly dead wrong) is that it
definitely needs to be supported directly - with PCI I don't believe
clones are allowed to "borrow" vendor or product ID codes, and this is how
BIOS and Linux determine which card it is.  This doesn't explain
occasional working/not working, but is a problem I've run into with PCI
NE2000 clones (for example Readylink RL2000).  Should be a case of copying
an entry in the table in pci.h (I think...), but I don't think that's your
problem...

2.) I have had sporadic detection of ISA NE2000 clones using the boot
kernels which have lots of different network driver support.  I've had so
much trouble with this, that when I'm building a Linux box for a customer
I keep a 3c509 around to boot onto, install Linux over the ethernet, then
rebuild the kernel for NE2000 and switch off to the NE2000.  I've
definitely had this problem appear and go away on successive reboots with
no hardware change at all, so this is my best guess for your problem. 

3.) It doesn't only work after booting Windoze and warm booting does it?

Good luck,

David
