>>the head faster, giving higher transfer rates, and the tracks are closer
>>together, for closer seeks.

>I'm not sure either.  I thought there was once a problem with speed
>because you had more space to look over when seeking?  I would really like
>to know if this is a factor.  Unless there is new technology in the
>bigger disks, wouldn't it be easier to find something on a 2.5 or 4 GB
>disk than a 7GB disk?  I don't know.

The amount of physical space to search is the same.  And when asking for
the information, the track & sector are known.  With sectors more
tightly packed, it is strictly faster  to move n tracks over; The
average time for the correct sector to pass under is the same, assuming
a random starting point, but the data is transferred at a strictly
faster rate, as it takes less time for the shorter sector to pass under
the head. 



