>> OUCH!  Uh, one of the HUGE reasons i love LINUX is because I can't afford
>> anything else that would be as powerful.  I know I can't afford SCSI right
>> now...  Are you offering to lend out the money to those of us who don't
>> have the resources for a SCSI system?  :)
>
>This is a joke, right?
>Man, the difference is $100 maximum.

   You've never priced high-end SCSI, have you?

   If you're starting at the ground floor, you'll be down $50-100 before
   you even start buying disks with SCSI (assuming that you buy a high-
   end controller.  An adaptec 1510 is certainly about as cheap as you
   can go, but when they start polling in sectors you'd be hard-pressed to
   tell the difference between one of them and a non-DMA IDE controller),
   and then disks run 10-40% more than the equivalent IDE disks.

   If you shop carefully, you can probably build a moderate system for
   around $100 more than an equivalent IDE system.  Whether it's worth
   it is another thing;  IDE is *just* *fine* for a workstation, and it
   works well for disk-intensive things on Linux as well (I've got
   workstations that have SCSI subsystems and workstations that use IDE;
   I can't tell the difference.  When I was working on WebShield for
   McAfee, I did some evaluation of SCSI vs IDE disks under heavy
   virus-scanning loads, and, at least under Linux with midrange gear
   [there is no high-end IDE hardware], the difference in performance
   was basically noise.  And my transit newsfeed puts its history file
   on an ide spindle, and I can't really see any difference between it
   and the other news machine, which lives on a pair of Ultra SCSI disks
   on separate BusLogic 948 controllers [and has an i80522/233 vs an AMD
   K5/90])

   SCSI really wins for me when I have to start dropping multiple
   devices onto a controller chain (IDE says it allows 2 devices, but
   for any sort of performance you need to stick with 1 device per
   chain) or if I'm running something that wants wads of bandwidth (like
   a software disk array) and that spends most of its life walking the
   disk.  And it's easier to configure.  But as for performance on a
   workstation?  If I didn't have a large stock in SCSI spares (or a
   steady stream of SCSI disks being rotated away from my main server as
   disk needs increase) I don't think I'd have a single SCSI workstation
   in my lab.


   And I've seen US$1000+ differences in the cost of large disk farms on
   IDE vs SCSI.  Thank god for the auction houses, because they bring
   the price down to merely US$100+

                 ____
   david parsons \bi/ SCSI is the superior choice, but it's not as obvious
                  \/                        as it was before busmaster IDE.



