Misc. comments:
Workstation performance is a mix of CPU, memory and disk performance. What we do
typically is compile and run our real applications and time them - so everything,
including compiler optimisation, is taken into account.
For Linux, DMA on disk I/O will double system performance. This used to mean SCSI;
now I think the Ultra DMA IDE is good, though it may not go 33MB/s (yet).
$$ spent on RAM is probably better than $$ spent on MHz, except for games or
code cracking.

You might consider multi-processor boards - dual or quad P II - for more performance.
MMX chips have more L1 cache, but the MMX instructions don't get you anywhere unless
you fix the compiler or code in assembler. Basically, they do quasi-parallel byte
arithmetic - good for masks in image manipulation or active filters in audio.

BTW, the Power PC 401e is a pretty good chip, I think. A quad 401 board comes out 
in front in the RC5 code cracking benchmarks, though that's strictly integer with a lot
of rotate left, so it favours CPUs that do ROTL in one cycle.
