http://math.vanderbilt.edu/~mayer/linux/results.html

A few comments about these benchmarks :

1- Even if the Alpha is first, his potential is even superior,
especially in floating points calculations. gcc still has to
improve. There are commercial C and Fortran compilers 
(http://www.microway.com).

2- The newest PowerPCs, which are much faster (604e/350, 750 with its
dedicated L2 bus, and the soon-to-appear and high-end 770) aren't
tested in this page, but even the older ones are very good, thanks to
the new gcc library written by Motorola. You should consider this
option : a PowerMac is cheaper than an Alpha or a Sparc.

3- There are no recent Sparcs tested in these page, but I tested some
recent UltraSparc at my labs, and the results are very disappointing
compared to other platforms. Nevertheless, calculations under Linux
are faster than calculations under Solaris. gcc on Sparc is not as
good as gcc on x86, but better than on other platforms.

4- gcc and g++ are always much better than the Fortan compiler g77.

5- egcs is the replacement project for gcc and its derivatives (g++, g77,
objective gcc), and its optimization should drastically improve. Even
on Intel platforms, for which gcc is excellent, you get 10 to 15%
better. I don't have results on other platforms.
(http://www.cygnus.com/egcs)

Well, to conclude, my only suggestion is : don't buy Sparc.

Intel : very low cost, mainstream (in particular, this is the only
platform on which you can get most commercial or non-free software,
such as the netscape browser, for example), huge community, maturity,
a lot of amazing and cheap hardware peripherals (3dfx voodoo chips for
3d imaging, for example, also available on PowerMacs but not well
supported), almost every hardware component has its driver.

Motorola PowerPC : low cost, good performances. Apple's charisma :),
IBM and Motorola's power and reliability.

Alpha 21164 or soon-to-come 21264 : amazing performances. 64 bit OS.
Digital's involvment in Linux development.

Alpha 21164PC (21164 with L2 out of chip, which involves lower cost
and a little loss in performances) : reasonable cost, excellent
performances. 64 bit OS. Production in volume should start right
now. Digital's involvment in Linux development.
