Greg Lindahl wrote:
> 
> jwest@jwest.ecen.okstate.edu (Jim West) writes:
> 
> > I'm wondering if an optimizing compiler sould pick this up even without the
> > explicit definition of nx_r. Can anyone explain why it wouldn't?
> 
> aliasing?
> 
Likely.  Some compilers will do a global aliasing check,
which is fairly time-consuming -- it helps to have
all the source in one file.  Most (I've used) make
conservative aliasing assumptions, on a function-by-
function basis.  Some compilers allow "no alias"
pragmas to be placed around critical functions, but this
practice is generally regarded as "cheating" for
benchmarks.  Others have a switch for global assumption
of no aliasing -- a rather dangerous option.

FORTRAN aficionados like to tout lack of aliasing as
a great advantage.  But I ask naively: what keeps a FORTRAN
function declared foo(xarray, yarray) from being called
foo(xarray, xarray)?



