The compiler is probably the culprit.  We don't know
how big your arrays are, so there is a chance you are thrashing
the cache with all that column-flipping, but I doubt it.  

About a year back we did some
comparisons among various architechtures and compilers, and
found: (1) g77 generally produced code a factor of 2 to 3 slower
than the commercial compilers (like Lahey on the PPro, DEC
FORTRAN on the alpha); for our memory-intensive tests, a 500
MHz DECalpha system was about 3 times faster than a 200 MHz
PPro.  Unfortunately, the tests were slightly skewed by the fact
that the alpha had 8 MB L3 on a total array set of about 12 MB,
but subsequent tests suggested at least a factor of 2.

On gcc code, one fellow did some "quick" gcc hand optimizations --
mainly stuffing important variables into registers -- and 
improved the alpha performance by nearly a factor of two.

You might request someone try to compile your code with
egcs-inspired g77.  I saw a note here recently (possibly
from Craig Burley?) that such a combination was possible.



