On Tue, 16 Sep 1997 17:49:03 -0700, Justin McNutt 
>So can that be done, or should I just give up (I *really* don't want to
>have to buy that CD)?

I just did an install from the RedHat CD, not because I had to, but
because someone had already bought it. If you want to install from a
local hard disk it should work fine, but it requires that you have a
local hard disk with RedHat copied onto it and it must be use a
filesystem known to Linux (ext2, FAT, vfat... NOT NTFS).

So, if your system has NT on it, create a big FAT partition and
download the latest version of RedHat onto it. Do the MILO thing to
boot a kernel and RAMDISK from floppies (after creating the floppies
on your NT system or elsewhere), then select the "harddisk" option
from the RedHat installation menu and you should be all set.

One quirk of the RedHat install is that the Alpha kernels don't seem
to support loadable modules. RedHat ungracefully pukes when it tries
to configure and use your network card.

Install RedHat without networking support,, then compile a new kernel
(I had a _really_ hard time just doing a kernel compile due to
internal compiler errors when trying to compile the Buslogic
Flashpoint driver. Disable the Flashpoint support when you configure
the kernel and it gets by the problem) with built in (* NOT M) support
for your network card, copy the compressed kernel image from
/usr/src/linux/arch/alpha/boot/vmlinux.gz to /vmlinux.gz and reboot.
When you compile the new kernel in /usr/src/linux, use "make boot" to
actually compile after doing a "make menuconfig" to configure it.

Good luck,

Luke



