torvalds@transmeta.com (Linus Torvalds) writes:

> Change them all to "long" and see whether that makes a difference. At
> least in theory gcc should have a much easier time with long, simply
> because then gcc is using only 64-bit quantities. 
> 
> With your current code, you're mixing 32-bit and 64-bit arithmetic,
> which limits the code generation a lot (because order matters when you
> mix different sizes).

Actually, we've already seen that the g77 generated code is more
restrictive than the f2c/gcc generated code. Yes, it's a difficult
problem, but it's extremely important to get this right. Presumably
Dec's compiler does, although it may not handle wrap-around
properly... you would only notice that for huge arrays.

> On a PPro you'll never see this, because everything is 32 bits, obviously, 

You might be able to similarly confuse gcc by using lots of shorts.

I once worked on a million line F66 program which broke the VMS optimizer
by using lots of INTEGER*2 variables. If you have different cases for
different sizes of things, you leave yourself open to having
a tiny bug in a rarely-used case...

-- g




