>are all "int" rather than "long"?

I presume he's updating a stream function or vorticity-driving fn, and
so will be REAL*4 or *8. Definitely *8 on the C90. Enough digression.

One simple thing--it has caught me before--he should check that the g77
code is giving the correct answers. If all machines are IEEE compliant
(the C90 won't be) all answers should be *very* close to each other.
They should be *identical* with -O0. g77 has some problems with
-f-move-all-movables; it can sometimes fail to see a dependancy within
a loop. Also, loop-unrolling can make a huge (x7) difference on alphas and
I vaguely remember gcc/g77 having some trouble with this a while ago.
Thusly, I concur with the others that g77 is the most likely culprit.
Using f2c + gcc + yaf will probably yield the same problems found with g77.
Time to spend  money on Nastran, NAG, Microway, or wait for the
re-worked g77. Good f77/90 is not Free (tm).

