I have a self-built system with the DEC-mainboard Vobis uses, so the basic 
hardware should be the same. The IDE-only version is ugly slow, because the
onboard IDE-controller doesn't support interrupts. You can use it for the CD-ROM,
but under WinNT the mouses freezes when reading big files. So SCSI for the
harddisks is a must...

|> A magazine article (c't Juli 97) mentioned that the Red Hat Linux
|> distribution was not running very stable.

In general, newer kernels (2.1.52) seem to be very stable, except the
IDE-driver... Also, some of the kernel error handling has problems, so you see a
lot of (non-fatal) panics, if you do something wrong (eg. mounting msdos-FS from
a 2K-sector medium or writing to a full msdos-FS...). I had some crashes when
trying a PCI-NE2000 ethernet card, but after some debugging (no, IO ports
shouldn't be short an Alpha :-) it runs very good (about 1.1MB/s isn't that bad
:-)

There is a flaw somewhere in the code, that gets time and date from the RTC. In
one out of five readouts, the year is set to 2017 or 2030, maybe the
processor is too fast when reading the RTC :-)
 
The other point is the RedHat distribution (I have 4.1), it caused  most of the
problems. The first is the bad description of the installation process of MILO
itself. It took me about 6 hours to figure out how to run MILO and the kernel.
Then the installation: If you are only planning to use the network,
don't say it in the installation! Otherwise the machine hangs at bootup. I think
(even with network) it is better to configure it after the system is running
stable. Some of the fresh installed programs crash with SIGSEGV, but if you 
compile them yourself, they work. Some of the problems should be solved with 
RedHat 4.2. I needed about a week until all worked (getting updates for amd,
XFree86 3.3 etc.)
BTW: RedHat comes without xfractint :-/

My hardware:
DEC PC164 mainboard
21164-P8 (533, but running at 500)
128MB (4*32MB, leaves room for upgrade and about 30% performance boost)
Matrox Millenium I 4MB
Symbios Logic 8600 (Ultra SCSI, is properly detected by the ARC-firmware)
Quantum Fireball Stratos 4.3GB (hdparm -t says ~5MB/s).
10Mbit PCI-Ethernetcard with Realtek RTL8029 (but 8390.c needs some patches...)
Kernel 2.1.52 with the BSD-ncr53xxx driver.
