On 22 Sep 1997 18:32:19 GMT, Donald K.Sinclair <dks@sgi9.hep.anl.gov> spake unto us:
>I just took delivery of an Alpha clone with Linux preinstalled. When I noticed
>that it had been configured with a 256 Mbyte swap partition, I queried the
>vendor, since it was my understanding that 128 Mbytes was the largest swap 
>partition that could be used, and that if one had a swap partition larger than
>128 Mbytes, only 128 Mbytes of it could ever be used. (For this reason I have
>2 128Mbyte swap partitions on my Linux Intel box.) The vendor claimed that
>they had configured systems with swap partitions as large as 1 Gbyte which
>appeared to be usable. Does this mean that there is a larger limit for the
>size of swap partitions on Linux Alpha than Linux Intel, or is the vendor in
>error?

According to discussion on axp-list, the limit on the Alpha is higher than
on Intel.  Apparently, the limit on swap partitions comes from the fact
that each swap partition is managed by a single page of physical RAM; since
on the Alpha pages are 8K instead of 4K, swap partitions can be larger.
How much larger, I don't know; 2, 4 or 8 times all seem like plausible
answers.

So, your vendor is correct.  Swap partitions on Alpha _can_ be larger than
they can on Intel.

See the axp-list archives at
http://www.redhat.com/support/mailing-lists/axp-list/
for more information.

