On 22 Sep 1997 14:53:51 GMT, jwest@jwest.ecen.okstate.edu (Jim West)
wrote:

>I'm wondering if an optimizing compiler sould pick this up even without the 
>explicit definition of nx_r. Can anyone explain why it wouldn't?

Well, it might all be unnecessary if we could gain the wisdom of
Robert's implementation :-)

The one thing I was trying to do was get as close to the FORTRAN
version's efficiency on the non-Alpha platforms. I succeeded on the
Pentium Pro and Pentium machines. One reason why is that the FORTRAN
code benefits greatly from hardcoding of dimensions. That is, if you
explicitly put the 1002,1002 numbers in the FORTRAN kernel, you get a
great (~20%) performance increase.

I think I am doing the same thing when I localize the reference to the
nx dimension in the C kernel by putting it into stack (and through
optimization into register). I did this after looking at the assembler
code in the two versions of the FORTRAN kernel, with and without the
hardcoded dimensions and found a lot of address resolution in one
where the other simply had a $### telling it how far to advance the
reference. I can't think of a way to explicitly code this same
approach in FORTRAN, though I am pretty sure that a lot of other
compilers do the same thing I did in the C kernel.

Luke



