: > I'm wondering if an optimizing compiler sould pick this up even without the 
: > explicit definition of nx_r. Can anyone explain why it wouldn't?

: aliasing?

: -- g

: (no, I didn't look closely, but such a small kernel routine
: in C is always at risk for aliasing problems...)

Yes, a C compiler may not cache the values, because in

  for (i=1;i<*nx-1;i++) {
    indx   = i+indj;
    psi_new[indx] = (*a1)*psi[indx+1]+(*a2)*psi[indx-1]+
                   (*a3)*psi[i+indjp1]+(*a4)*psi[i+indjm1]-
                   (*a5)*f[indx];
  }

the write to psi_new[indx] could modify all the other values.
When the restrict keyword will be supported in a future C standard
this could be avoided.

Volker



