torvalds@transmeta.com (Linus Torvalds) writes:

> The problem was due to the newer 'malloc()' implementation that no
> longer allocated contiguous memory (it uses anonymous mmap() for large
> memory allocations).  Quake uses some 32-bit offsets internally, and the
> offsets between different allocations no longer fit into 32 bits with
> the new malloc.

This is true, probably because the kernel picks addresses far away
from the `conventional' heap for anonymous mmap(0, ...).  I guess no
application should depend on small offsets for 64bit systems.

Please take note that a quick solution for this kind of problem
(without compiling a new binary) is to disable the use of mmap() in
the allocator by setting the environment variable
`MALLOC_MMAP_MAX_=0'.  This works on any recent GNU libc-2 (and also
with Linux libc-5.4.x), and is IMHO _much_ preferable to preloading
another libc or malloc.

The same holds for buggy applications that e.g. free() a pointer twice
in a row -- in many cases this can be caught by setting
`MALLOC_CHECK_=1' (at some performance cost; please do fix the
applications in the long run).

I hope this helps preventing some panic and embarassment in the
future...

Regards,
Wolfram.



