In article <3421C55E.701BAF8E@octoraro.org>, Andrew Smith wrote:
>Hi All,
>
>I just got a Multia/UDB 166mhz Alpha to run Linux on, and now I need to
>get some memory for it.
>
>My question is, what kind do I need to get?  Does it require parity
>SIMM's?  If I install two 16mb parity SIMM's will it work okay?  Can I
>use non-parity?

You must use _true_parity_ SIMMs added in pairs.  Unfortunately, many
of the folks hawking memory these days try to pass off "logic parity"
SIMM modules as parity memory.  Most truly don't understand the
difference; giving weight to the joke about:

Q.

"What's the difference between a computer products salesperson and a
used-car salesperson?"

A.

"The used-car salesperson knows when he's lying to you.."

Seriously, I would deal ONLY with reputable outfits.  My personal
favorite is The Chip Merchant in San Diego (www.thechipmerchant.com).
Their prices are just about the lowest you will find, and they do not
even carry logic-parity memory.

I just paid $130.00 for a pair of 16Meg 60nsec. parity simms for my
UDB.

Steve





