On 19 Sep 1997 08:12:06 GMT, current@plains.NoDak.edu (Robert W
Current) wrote:
>I am shopping around, and I want to get a new box.  Thing is I have IDE
>drives, floppy drives, keys, mouse... for a x86, but I am interested in
>this group because PowerPC is RISC based (or is it? I don't really know,
>just heard a rumer and haven't really read much on it).

If that's what you know at this point, then it's probably wiser for
you to go with an x86-based system.

>So, based on rumor alone, I hear RISC should be better than an x86 style
>system, but I don't know.  I was going to get a K6 233 (now LINUX bug
>free) but I wanted to get a first hand opinion on if PowerPC might be
>better...  So, are there any direct comparisons?  How is hardware
>compatability?  What would be diffrent for x86 vs. PowerPC in LINUX (all
>software compatable? opperation times? What really is the diffrence?)? 

A RISC processor can provide faster performance than an x86 processor;
milage can vary quite highly based on such things as:

a) Are you comparing last year's RISC to this year's x86?

If you take an old PowerMac and install Linux on it, it isn't likely
that it will give substantially better performance than the latest AMD
chip.

b) Do you have a properly configured RISC box?

It is common for RISC systems to be more "RAM hungry" than CISC
systems.  I can't speak for PPC systems, but Alpha-based systems need
to have fairly large amounts of cache in order to perform well.

It makes little sense to buy a RISC system costing much less than $4K
USD, as with anything less, you're going to be making substantial
hardware compromises that are likely to compromise overall
performance.

For $4K, you'll be getting:
- Fast SCSI
- Quite a lot of RAM
- A *good* motherboard
- Fast graphics

and this will be quite suited to CPU-hungry processing.

c) Is your processing actually CPU dependent?

- Raytracing is
- Numerical applications are
- Heavy data encryption applications are

But.

- Web serving usually isn't
- Web browsing isn't
- Reading news isn't
- Sending/receiving mail isn't

If you don't know already that your processing is CPU bound, it's
highly probable that it isn't.

The major alternatives are:
a) I/O bound
Database, news, and web processing is commonly bound by the speed of
your disk system.  In such cases, getting a faster disk drive is the
only way to improve performance.

b) User bound
If the system is waiting for you to click on something, it's waiting
for you, and speeding up the CPU is of absolutely no value at such
times.

c) Memory speed bound
These days, processors are seriously a lot faster than the memory that
they access.  A 500MHz Alpha chip, for instance, can access memory
25-30 times faster than 50-60ns SIMM modules can provide data.
Even if the process *is* CPU intensive, it's not difficult for the CPU
to "outrun" the capabilities of RAM to provide data.  Digital "Multia"
systems are particularly afflicted in this fashion; they have small
caches which have the result that for some processing they perform
like a P75, and for other processing they are reputed to outperform a
P200.  (I'd like to test this on my Multia; haven't found the
benchmarks yet.)

At this point in time, a new user is likely better off looking at an
x86 version of Linux.  The variants for other architectures are
getting more robust all the time, but there isn't much commercial
software for 'em, and there tend to be more problems getting Linux to
install on non-x86 systems.

Perhaps the best overview on how to configure a Linux box can be found
at:

<A
HREF="http://www.ccil.org/~esr/writings/lj-howtobuild.html">Building
the Perfect Box: How to Design Your Linux Workstation,</A> written by
Eric Raymond.  

It contains some essential principles that hold very true, despite the
fact that there have been some hardware improvements since the time at
which it was written.

For instance, it is entirely possible that you may find an "obsolete"
P166 will fully satisfy your processing needs.
