Brian,
    I had similar problems with an AlphaStation 250.  It is probably 
because yp stuff is set up.  If you can disable it everything should go
back to normal.   Your best bet is to boot with the redhat boot disk, flip
to the second console (alt-f2), mount your root device, look in /etc/rc.d,
/etc/sysconfig or something like that, and deleted all references to yp
services.  If you need to use the yp stuff, you can reenable it once you
get hooked up to the network. 

In my opinion this is really a big bug and something that should be fixed.
All the subsystems might be operating correctly, but it is a real pain in
the a** for the user.  A computer should NOT be unusable if it is off the
net (or misconfigured).   It seems, however, that many UNIXs suffer from
that problem. (Digital Unix at least.)   If Unix/Linux is going to
compete with
Windows, it has to be somewhat tolerant of misconfigured systems.  Sorry,
I'm off my soapbox now.  


Hope that helps,
Phil 



