Don't get me wrong here.  I love linux/unix and I use it nearly
exclusively on all of my machines, but there has been too many times (on
both my intel
and alpha box), when I yank the network connector and the next time I
boot up the machine crawls along.  It may be easy to find the problem
and fix it, but until that is done the machine is in an almost unusable
state.   When you don't quite know what is wrong and want to do a little
trial and error to try to fix it, the terrible response time makes it
next to impossible.  

You are right when you say that windows is unstable and crashes left and
right, however, I have never had a problem when I yanked the net connection
and turned it on.  I might not be able to telnet/ftp/use netscape, but the
rest of the system was running well enough to be able to fix the problem.

You might say "use a bootdisk."  I say "Why should I have to?"  Just
because the net goes down shouldn't mean that the system should become
unusable.

Given the choice between windows/Linux, I would pick Linux hands down, but
there are still some warts, and I think that this is one of them.

Phil 



