Brian wrote:
> 
> Gang,
>         I have a Multia Alpha running linux (duh).  It takes about 30 to 45
> minutes to boot linux.  The build looks good and the thing works fine after
> boot up? is this normal?
> Is there anything I can check to speed the boot process up.  I have it
> configured for network and it is not yet connected to the network, could
> this be it?

That's it.

If you've got networking configured the Multia hangs at the "eth 0:
media is TP" and waits forever for the gateway to respond. If you don't
always network you can make a script to set up static routes and run
standalone without the wait. Here's my net.setup script;

/sbin/ifconfig eth0 127.0.0.1 (whatever your IP address is)
/sbin/route add 127.0.0.2 (your gateway address)
/sbin/route add default gw 127.0.0.2 eth0 (your gateway of course)

chmod 700 net.setup

Save your existing /etc/resolv.conf.

Now when you boot do a ./net.setup and your network is up.

Don't ask me how to disable your existing network setup. I did mine
manually by editing what seemed like every other file in /etc. Maybe
netconfig can do it.

Good luck,
Charles



